---
import { REPO_PATH, REPO_URL } from '../lib/site.mjs';
---

{/*
  The colophon, hoisted out of the two pages that used to carry it.

  The markup was duplicated byte-for-byte in `[...slug].astro` and `404.astro`,
  which was survivable while both cells were static text and stopped being
  survivable the moment one of them gained a link: a two-file edit with no test
  between them is a permanent-divergence generator. One component, one truth.

  The third cell is the repository record, and it rides with `Compiled 2026-09-22`
  rather than beside the title — which is the register the book already insists on.
  `07-appendix-sources/02-version-drift-survival.md` states the rule: a repository's
  licence, last push and archive flag are properties of a moment, so print them with
  the date they were read. A bare GitHub button in chrome would be a repository fact
  carrying no date at all.

  No hit-target utilities here, and that is deliberate. This is a link inside a line
  of text, not a standalone control, and WCAG 2.5.8 exempts targets in a block of
  text. `hit-targets.test.mjs` counts `min-h-6`/`min-w-6` as the four STANDALONE
  links a reader navigates by; putting a fifth utility on an in-text link would
  widen that claim to something the design does not mean.
*/}
<footer class="mt-auto border-t border-rule">
  <div class="mx-auto flex w-shell flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 py-8">
    <p class="meta m-0">Oracle SQL Optimization · Evidence-graded notebook</p>
    <p class="meta m-0">Compiled 2026-09-22 · No live DB</p>
    <p class="meta m-0">
      <a
        href={REPO_URL}
        class="inline-block transition-colors hover:text-accent focus-visible:text-accent"
      >
        Repo · {REPO_PATH}
      </a>
    </p>
  </div>
</footer>
